("Two sibling components each calling `useSuspenseQuery` *within the same render pass*: React renders the first, it suspends... In the classic model React stops at the first suspension in a boundary, so the sibling's request would not start until the first resolves, producing a **waterfall caused by Suspense itself**. Remedies: start requests before render (prefetch / loaders), use `useSuspenseQueries` for known parallel queries, or put siblings in separate boundaries. ⚠ React 19.x revised how siblings of a suspended component are scheduled and added pre-warming behaviour in later 19 minors (verify the Suspense reference and release notes for your exact version); do not rely on sibling pre-rendering for performance.",
 "The waterfalls that remain are **sequential reads inside one component** (the component suspends at the first read, so the second read never executes until the first resolves) and **parent-then-child** chains (a child only renders, and so only starts its request, after its suspended parent resolves). *Sibling* components are different in React 19: when a component suspends, React commits the nearest fallback immediately and then renders the suspended siblings to \"pre-warm\" their requests (React 19 upgrade guide, \"Improvements to Suspense\"). Verified on React 19.3 with a render-time promise cache: `<Suspense><A/><B/></Suspense>` where each calls `use(load(key))` starts *both* requests within a few ms, while `Parent` that `use`s a promise and then renders `Child` (which starts its own request) starts the child request only after the parent's promise resolves (~150 ms later in the experiment). React 18 stopped at the first suspension, so older articles describe a sibling waterfall that React 19 mostly removes. Remedies still apply for the cases above: start requests before render (prefetch / loaders), use `useSuspenseQueries` for known parallel queries inside one component, and avoid nested parent-then-child fetches. Do not *rely* on pre-warming as your parallelism strategy; it only helps siblings that are already in the tree."),
